## 3. How the Plugin Learns About Agents & Processes

### Data Sources

The plugin reads **three projection files** (the daemon writes these):

1. **`/host/.panopt/todos.md`**
   - Format: `- [ ] [#<id>](todos/<id>.md) <title> - <status>, <priority>, updated <ts>`
   - Parsed by `parse_index_line()` (panopt-zellij-logic:628)
   - Yields: `Vec<(u64, String)>` where String is the raw label incl. suffix

2. **`/host/.panopt/notes.md`**
   - Same format as todos

3. **`/host/.panopt/processes.md`**
   - Format: `- [<kind>] #<id> <label> [(from #<source-id>)]`
   - Example: `- [agent] #1 NASTL-Mediator`
   - Parsed by `parse_process_line()` (panopt-zellij-logic:650)
   - Yields: `Vec<ProcessRow>` with `kind` (`"agent"`, `"command"`, `"terminal"`) and `label`

4. **`/host/.panopt/.cockpit/agent-labels.json`**
   - Projection of agent pane → user-supplied label mappings
   - Written by plugin itself (line 1160: `write_agent_labels(&self.agent_labels)`)
   - Read by all five instances (line 1168: `read_agent_labels()`)
   - Format: `{"<terminal_pane_id>":"<label>",...}`
   - Parsed by `parse_agent_labels()` (panopt-zellij-logic:448)

### No MCP Tool Calls from Plugin

The plugin does **NOT** call MCP tools to discover agents/processes. It relies entirely on reading the projection files.

**Only the daemon calls MCP tools** (via `panopt` CLI subcommands):
- `panopt _agent-leave --id <id>` — agent death cleanup (line 1145) ← This is a **background `run_command` call**, not blocking

**Other subcommands called**:
- `panopt todo rm <id>` (line 1762)
- `panopt note rm <id>` (line 1765)
- `panopt process delete <id>` (line 1776)
- `panopt search` (spawned in popup, line 1641)
- `panopt _viewer` (spawned in slot, line 1890)
- `panopt _agent` (spawned in slot, line 2013)
- `panopt _process-run <id>` (spawned in slot, line 1843)

